<div class="eo14-body">
<div class="eo14-main">

  <!-- ═══ SUMMARY ═══ -->
  <div id="eo14-panel-summary" class="eo14-panel active">
    <div class="sec14">
      <div class="pc14">
        <div class="pc14-lbl">Plain Language Summary</div>
        <div class="pc14-txt">President Trump signed Executive Order 14404 on May 1, 2026. It does two major things: (1) it creates a new US government authority to sanction any foreign person or company that operates in five key sectors of Cuba's economy — energy, defense, metals and mining, financial services, and security; and (2) it threatens foreign banks and financial institutions with losing access to the US financial system if they process significant transactions for anyone sanctioned under this order. This is called a "secondary sanctions" regime — it goes beyond the existing US embargo on Cuba (the CACR) to put pressure on non-American companies doing business in Cuba.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sec14">
      <div class="nh-ey14">Why This Order Exists</div>
      <h2 class="nh-hl14">Maximum Pressure — Extended to the World</h2>
      <p class="nh-ld14">The US has maintained a comprehensive embargo on Cuba since 1962. That embargo (the Cuban Assets Control Regulations, CACR) only constrains US persons and entities. E.O. 14404 adds a second layer: it uses the International Emergency Economic Powers Act to threaten foreign companies and foreign banks with US sanctions if they do significant business with Cuba's sanctioned sectors. The model is borrowed directly from US secondary sanctions against Russia and Iran.</p>
      <div class="nc14">
        <span class="nc14-ic">⚖️</span>
        <span class="nc14-tx">E.O. 14404 does NOT replace the existing Cuba embargo (CACR). It sits on top of it. The CACR continues to apply to US persons and entities. E.O. 14404 adds new designation authority for foreign persons and creates secondary sanctions risk for foreign financial institutions — even those with no prior US connection to Cuba.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="qc14">
        <div class="qc14-tx">"Cuba is controlled by GAESA — a 'state within a state' that is accountable to no one and hoards the profits from its businesses for the benefit of a small elite."</div>
        <div class="qc14-at">Secretary of State Marco Rubio · announcing first E.O. 14404 designations · May 7, 2026</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sdiv14"><span class="sdiv14-in">✦ ✦ ✦</span></div>
      <div class="nh-ey14">Context · What Came Before</div>
      <h2 class="nh-hl14">A Campaign, Not a Single Act</h2>
      <p class="nh-ld14">E.O. 14404 is the most significant action in a series of escalating Cuba-related measures beginning at the start of Trump's second term. It follows the re-issuance of NSPM-5 in June 2025, which reimposed restrictions Obama had lifted, and a series of fuel supply disruptions targeting Cuba's energy imports.</p>
      <div class="pr14">
        <div class="pr14-n">①</div>
        <div class="pr14-c">
          <div class="pr14-t">Cuba Assets Control Regulations (CACR) — The Original Embargo</div>
          <div class="pr14-x">In place since 1962. Prohibits US persons from trading with, investing in, or providing financial services to Cuba. Comprehensive but limited to US persons. E.O. 14404 adds on top.</div>
          <div class="pr14-b"><span class="badge14 b-ofac">OFAC Administered</span><span class="badge14 b-grey">Since 1962</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pr14">
        <div class="pr14-n">②</div>
        <div class="pr14-c">
          <div class="pr14-t">NSPM-5 Reissued — June 2025</div>
          <div class="pr14-x">Trump reissued and amended National Security Presidential Memorandum 5, reinstating restrictions on US tourism and financial flows to Cuba that Obama had loosened. This tightened the primary embargo before 14404 added the secondary layer.</div>
          <div class="pr14-b"><span class="badge14 b-grey">Jun 2025</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pr14">
        <div class="pr14-n">③</div>
        <div class="pr14-c">
          <div class="pr14-t">E.O. 14404 — The Secondary Sanctions Layer</div>
          <div class="pr14-x">Signed May 1, 2026. Creates IEEPA-based designation authority for foreign persons. Adds secondary sanctions risk for foreign financial institutions. First designations issued May 7, 2026 — GAESA, Ania Guillermina Lastres, and Moa Nickel S.A.</div>
          <div class="pr14-b"><span class="badge14 b-new">New Authority</span><span class="badge14 b-ieepa">IEEPA</span><span class="badge14 b-sec">Secondary Sanctions</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ KEY PROVISIONS ═══ -->
  <div id="eo14-panel-provisions" class="eo14-panel">
    <div class="sec14">
      <div class="nh-ey14">Provisions · What the Order Actually Does</div>
      <h2 class="nh-hl14">Two Authorities, One Order</h2>
      <p class="nh-ld14">E.O. 14404 grants the Treasury Secretary and Secretary of State two distinct new powers. The first targets foreign persons in specific Cuban economic sectors. The second targets foreign financial institutions that process transactions for those persons. Together they internationalize US pressure on Cuba beyond what the CACR can reach.</p>
      <div class="pr14">
        <div class="pr14-n">1</div>
        <div class="pr14-c">
          <div class="pr14-t">Sectoral Designation Authority — Foreign Persons</div>
          <div class="pr14-x">Authorizes blocking sanctions on any foreign person determined to operate in any of five specified sectors of the Cuban economy. The US can now sanction a Spanish hotel chain, a Canadian mining company, or a Chinese port operator if it operates in a targeted sector — without those companies violating any US rule.</div>
          <div class="pr14-b"><span class="badge14 b-new">New · No Prior Equivalent</span><span class="badge14 b-ieepa">IEEPA Authority</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pr14">
        <div class="pr14-n">2</div>
        <div class="pr14-c">
          <div class="pr14-t">Secondary Sanctions on Foreign Financial Institutions (FFIs)</div>
          <div class="pr14-x">Any foreign bank that processes "significant" transactions for anyone designated under E.O. 14404 may itself be designated — cutting it off from US correspondent accounts and the dollar payment system. This mirrors the Russia E.O. 14114 (2023) model. The FFI does not need to know the transaction is prohibited; significant transactions with blocked parties create exposure.</div>
          <div class="pr14-b"><span class="badge14 b-sec">Secondary Sanctions</span><span class="badge14 b-new">Extraterritorial Reach</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sdiv14"><span class="sdiv14-in">✦ ✦ ✦</span></div>
      <div class="nh-ey14">Five Targeted Sectors</div>
      <h2 class="nh-hl14">Where the Exposure Lies</h2>
      <p class="nh-ld14">E.O. 14404 names five sectors where foreign persons face designation risk. Treasury may add sectors in consultation with State. OFAC's FAQ 1256 clarified that operating in these sectors creates elevated sanctions risk.</p>
      <div class="sg14">
        <div class="sc14"><div class="sc14-ic">⚡</div><div class="sc14-nm">Energy</div><div class="sc14-tx">Oil, gas, electricity generation, and fuel distribution. Cuba's acute fuel crisis makes this sector politically central. Venezuela's withdrawal as a supplier created openings for new international suppliers — all now at risk.</div><span class="sc14-rk rk-hi">High Exposure</span></div>
        <div class="sc14"><div class="sc14-ic">⚔️</div><div class="sc14-nm">Defense &amp; Related Materiel</div><div class="sc14-tx">Military equipment, security apparatus support, weapons, and materiel. Directly targets entities that supply or service the Cuban Revolutionary Armed Forces (FAR) — which controls GAESA.</div><span class="sc14-rk rk-hi">High Exposure</span></div>
        <div class="sc14"><div class="sc14-ic">⛏️</div><div class="sc14-nm">Metals &amp; Mining</div><div class="sc14-tx">Cuba has significant nickel and cobalt reserves. Moa Nickel S.A. — a Sherritt International joint venture with Cuban state — was designated May 7. Sherritt subsequently suspended operations.</div><span class="sc14-rk rk-hi">High Exposure · Moa Nickel Designated</span></div>
        <div class="sc14"><div class="sc14-ic">🏦</div><div class="sc14-nm">Financial Services</div><div class="sc14-tx">Banking, payments, trade finance, remittances. GAESA was designated under this sector on May 7. Any foreign bank processing payments for GAESA or its subsidiaries faces secondary sanctions risk.</div><span class="sc14-rk rk-xt">Critical · GAESA Designated</span></div>
        <div class="sc14"><div class="sc14-ic">🔒</div><div class="sc14-nm">Security Sector</div><div class="sc14-tx">Cuba's Ministry of the Interior and security apparatus — designated May 18. Targets surveillance technology providers, security trainers, and intelligence service supporters.</div><span class="sc14-rk rk-md">Medium · MINFAR Designated May 18</span></div>
        <div class="sc14"><div class="sc14-ic">➕</div><div class="sc14-nm">Additional Sectors (TBD)</div><div class="sc14-tx">Treasury may designate additional sectors in consultation with State. Tourism is the most frequently cited candidate given GAESA's hotel empire, but has not been formally added as of June 2026.</div><span class="sc14-rk rk-md">Watch · Tourism Likely Next</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ DESIGNATIONS ═══ -->
  <div id="eo14-panel-designations" class="eo14-panel">
    <div class="sec14">
      <div class="nh-ey14">SDN Designations · Persons &amp; Entities Named</div>
      <h2 class="nh-hl14">Who Has Been Designated Under E.O. 14404</h2>
      <p class="nh-ld14">Designations are issued by the State Department and administered by Treasury OFAC. The first wave came May 7 — six days after signing. A second wave came May 18. The State Department has indicated additional designations are ongoing.</p>
      <table class="sdn14">
        <thead><tr><th>Entity / Person</th><th>Type</th><th>Sector</th><th>Date</th><th>Key Fact</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><div class="sdn14-nm">GAESA — Grupo de Administración Empresarial S.A.</div><div class="sdn14-sb">Cuban military business conglomerate · ~37–40% of Cuba's GDP</div></td>
            <td><span class="sdn14-tp sdn14-en">Entity</span></td>
            <td><div style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:7.5px;color:var(--ink-mut);">Financial Services</div></td>
            <td><div class="sdn14-dt">May 7, 2026</div></td>
            <td><div style="font-family:'Nunito',sans-serif;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--ink-soft);">Already SDN under CACR since Dec 2020. 14404 adds secondary sanctions risk for foreign parties dealing with it.</div></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><div class="sdn14-nm">Ania Guillermina Lastres Morera</div><div class="sdn14-sb">Executive President of GAESA · Brigadier General</div></td>
