  <!-- ═══ WHO IT AFFECTS ═══ -->
  <div id="eo14-panel-impact" class="eo14-panel">
    <div class="sec14">
      <div class="nh-ey14">Impact · Who Is Affected and How</div>
      <h2 class="nh-hl14">An Order That Reaches Far Beyond the United States</h2>
      <p class="nh-ld14">E.O. 14404's secondary sanctions regime is explicitly designed to affect non-American parties. Any non-US company doing significant business in Cuba's targeted sectors risks losing access to the US financial system. This is how the US enforces Cuba policy on the world, not just on Americans.</p>
      <div class="ag14">
        <div class="ac14"><div class="ac14-fl">🇪🇸🇩🇪🇮🇹🇫🇷</div><div class="ac14-nm">European Companies &amp; Banks</div><span class="ac14-im ai-hi">High Exposure</span><div class="ac14-tx">European banks processing transactions linked to GAESA or designated entities risk losing US correspondent accounts — effectively exclusion from the dollar payment circuit. Spanish hotel chains operating Gaviota properties are in the direct line of exposure. Bird &amp; Bird advised that "transaction screening due diligence must be immediate and on an ongoing basis."</div></div>
        <div class="ac14"><div class="ac14-fl">🇨🇦</div><div class="ac14-nm">Canadian Companies</div><span class="ac14-im ai-hi">Already Hit — Sherritt</span><div class="ac14-tx">Sherritt International's Moa Nickel S.A. joint venture was designated May 7. Sherritt suspended operations. Canada has historically been one of Cuba's most significant trade and mining partners — and faces the sharpest immediate impact of any non-American country from E.O. 14404.</div></div>
        <div class="ac14"><div class="ac14-fl">🇨🇳</div><div class="ac14-nm">China</div><span class="ac14-im ai-md">Strategic Exposure</span><div class="ac14-tx">China has dramatically expanded Cuba investment — solar parks, port infrastructure, financial agreements. Chinese banks processing transactions for GAESA-linked entities face secondary sanctions risk. Whether the US would actually designate a major Chinese state bank is a separate political calculation — but the legal authority now exists under 14404.</div></div>
        <div class="ac14"><div class="ac14-fl">🇷🇺</div><div class="ac14-nm">Russia</div><span class="ac14-im ai-md">Already Sanctioned Context</span><div class="ac14-tx">Russia is already broadly sanctioned. Its Cuba engagement continues through mechanisms already outside the dollar system. 14404 adds marginal additional pressure, but Russia's Cuba-related transactions were largely outside US reach before the order.</div></div>
        <div class="ac14"><div class="ac14-fl">🇨🇺</div><div class="ac14-nm">Cuban People</div><span class="ac14-im ai-hi">Indirect · Acute</span><div class="ac14-tx">The Cuban government argues E.O. 14404 harms ordinary Cubans, not the regime. The US argues GAESA's extraction of national wealth is what harms ordinary Cubans, and 14404 is designed to stop that extraction. Both things may be simultaneously true — and the Cuban people absorb the costs. Remittance access and food import channels are at risk from secondary sanctions "de-risking" by foreign banks.</div></div>
        <div class="ac14"><div class="ac14-fl">🇺🇸</div><div class="ac14-nm">Cuban-Americans</div><span class="ac14-im ai-md">Remittances at Risk</span><div class="ac14-tx">Cuban-Americans send billions annually to family on the island. As foreign banks de-risk to avoid 14404 exposure, the channels through which remittances flow to Cuba narrow — cutting a lifeline for ordinary Cubans whose families are abroad.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sdiv14"><span class="sdiv14-in">✦ ✦ ✦</span></div>
      <div class="nh-ey14">Unintended Effects · What the Order May Cause</div>
      <h2 class="nh-hl14">Documented Concerns from Legal and Economic Analysts</h2>
      <p class="nh-ld14">Multiple law firms and policy analysts have documented concerns that E.O. 14404's secondary sanctions may produce effects beyond the intended targets.</p>
      <div class="pr14"><div class="pr14-n">①</div><div class="pr14-c"><div class="pr14-t">De-risking of Foreign Banks Cuts Remittance Channels</div><div class="pr14-x">Foreign financial institutions, fearing secondary sanctions exposure, may exit Cuba-related business entirely — including remittance processing. This would reduce the flow of money from the US Cuban diaspora to family members on the island, disproportionately hurting ordinary Cubans.</div></div></div>
      <div class="pr14"><div class="pr14-n">②</div><div class="pr14-c"><div class="pr14-t">Accelerated Chinese Economic Dominance in Cuba</div><div class="pr14-x">If European and Canadian companies exit Cuba to avoid 14404 exposure, China — which already dominates Cuba's solar, port, and biotech sectors — gains market share by default. The order may accelerate exactly the Chinese strategic positioning in Cuba that concerns US national security planners.</div></div></div>
      <div class="pr14"><div class="pr14-n">③</div><div class="pr14-c"><div class="pr14-t">Tourism Sector Impact — Cubans in Hospitality Lose Wages</div><div class="pr14-x">If GAESA hotel designation causes foreign tour operators and booking platforms to stop selling Cuba packages, the hospitality sector — which employs tens of thousands of Cubans — loses revenue. Whether this pressure reaches GAESA's overseas accounts or ordinary Cuban workers first is contested.</div></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ TIMELINE ═══ -->
  <div id="eo14-panel-timeline" class="eo14-panel">
    <div class="sec14">
      <div class="nh-ey14">Timeline · Order Issuance Through June 2026</div>
      <h2 class="nh-hl14">What Happened and When</h2>
      <div class="tl14">
        <div class="tl14-it"><div class="tl14-dt amber"></div><div class="tl14-da">June 2025</div><div class="tl14-ti">NSPM-5 Reissued — Tightening Primary Embargo</div><div class="tl14-tx">Trump reissues and amends National Security Presidential Memorandum 5, reinstating Obama-era rollbacks. Venezuela oil supply to Cuba begins deteriorating. Cuba enters fuel crisis.</div></div>
        <div class="tl14-it"><div class="tl14-dt amber"></div><div class="tl14-da">January 2026</div><div class="tl14-ti">Trump Blocks Fuel Deliveries · Venezuelan Oil Ends</div><div class="tl14-tx">US pressure on fuel suppliers intensifies. Venezuela's political arrangement collapses, ending cheap oil to Cuba. Mexico temporarily halts shipments under US pressure. Cuba declares fuel emergency. China steps in with $80M emergency aid.</div></div>
        <div class="tl14-it"><div class="tl14-dt red"></div><div class="tl14-da">May 1, 2026</div><div class="tl14-ti">E.O. 14404 Signed</div><div class="tl14-tx">President Trump signs E.O. 14404. Grounded in IEEPA, NEA, INA §212(f). Creates new IEEPA-based Cuba sanctions program separate from CACR. White House simultaneously publishes fact sheet.</div></div>
        <div class="tl14-it"><div class="tl14-dt red"></div><div class="tl14-da">May 7, 2026</div><div class="tl14-ti">First Designations — GAESA, Lastres, Moa Nickel</div><div class="tl14-tx">State Department designates GAESA, Ania Guillermina Lastres Morera, and Moa Nickel S.A. under E.O. 14404. OFAC issues General License and FAQ guidance. Wind-down period through June 5 for GAESA transactions — now expired.</div></div>
        <div class="tl14-it"><div class="tl14-dt red"></div><div class="tl14-da">May 18, 2026</div><div class="tl14-ti">Second Wave — MINFAR, Security Network</div><div class="tl14-tx">State Department designates MINFAR, a gold mining joint venture, 5 Cuban entities, and 5 individuals connected to Cuba's extraterritorial influence network. Rubio: "I am also designating a Cuban government gold mining joint venture that continues to enrich the Cuban military and elite at the expense of its people."</div></div>
        <div class="tl14-it"><div class="tl14-dt amber"></div><div class="tl14-da">June 5, 2026</div><div class="tl14-ti">GAESA Wind-Down Period Expires</div><div class="tl14-tx">OFAC's limited non-targeting posture for GAESA wind-down transactions expires. All transactions with GAESA and GAESA 50%+ subsidiaries now carry full secondary sanctions exposure for foreign persons.</div></div>
        <div class="tl14-it"><div class="tl14-dt green"></div><div class="tl14-da">June 12–18, 2026</div><div class="tl14-ti">Cuba Announces Economic Reforms — Possible Response</div><div class="tl14-tx">Díaz-Canel announces sweeping economic reform package June 12 (later unanimously approved June 18). Cuba's Communist Party approves unprecedented privatization measures. Cuban officials state the decision is "not related to negotiations" with the US — but the timing follows the peak E.O. 14404 pressure period.</div></div>
        <div class="tl14-it"><div class="tl14-dt" style="background:var(--blue-lt);"></div><div class="tl14-da">Ongoing · June 2026</div><div class="tl14-ti">Additional Designations Expected · Tourism Watch</div><div class="tl14-tx">State Department has signaled ongoing designations. Tourism sector is the most frequently cited next target given GAESA's control of Cuba's hotel industry. FORA tracks new designations as they are announced.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══ DISCOURSE ═══ -->
  <div id="eo14-panel-discourse" class="eo14-panel">
    <div class="sec14">
      <div class="nh-ey14">Discourse · Community Perspectives</div>
      <h2 class="nh-hl14">What Do You Think?</h2>
      <div class="dp14">
        <div class="dp14-lb">Civic Question · E.O. 14404</div>
        <div class="dp14-q">Does secondary sanctions pressure on foreign companies doing business in Cuba help free the Cuban people — or does it hurt them?</div>
        <div class="dp14-ac"><button class="dp14-btn dp14-co">Helps · 1,203</button><button class="dp14-btn dp14-ob">Hurts · 847</button></div>
      </div>
      <div class="dp14" style="margin-top:14px;">
        <div class="dp14-lb">Civic Question · GAESA Designation</div>
        <div class="dp14-q">Sanctioning GAESA is intended to stop the military elite from extracting Cuba's wealth. But GAESA also controls remittance channels, stores, and banks that ordinary Cubans depend on. Does this designation help or harm the Cuban people first?</div>
        <div class="dp14-ac"><button class="dp14-btn dp14-co">Helps · 892</button><button class="dp14-btn dp14-ob">Harms · 1,104</button></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sdiv14" style="margin-top:24px;"><span class="sdiv14-in">✦ ✦ ✦</span></div>
      <div class="dc14">
        <div class="dc14-hd"><div class="dc14-av">MI</div><div class="dc14-hn">@miami.investor.2026</div><span class="dc14-bg">Cuban-American</span></div>
        <div class="dc14-tx">GAESA has been stealing from the Cuban people since the 1990s. An $18 billion slush fund while people can't find cooking oil. The designation is decades overdue. The Cuban people will not be free until the military's grip on the economy is broken. This order is a step toward that.</div>
        <div class="dc14-ac"><button class="dc14-btn">Concur 341</button><button class="dc14-btn">Reply</button><button class="dc14-btn">Object 112</button></div>
      </div>
      <div class="dc14">
        <div class="dc14-hd"><div class="dc14-av" style="background:var(--green);">CH</div><div class="dc14-hn">@cuba.havana.resident</div><span class="dc14-bg" style="background:var(--red-p);border-color:var(--red-r);color:var(--red);">On the Island</span></div>
        <div class="dc14-tx">The store where I buy rice is TRD Caribe. That's GAESA. The bank where my cousin sends remittances goes through GAESA infrastructure. When you sanction GAESA, you're sanctioning the store and the remittance. I understand why the US wants to pressure the regime. But the pressure lands here, on us, before it ever reaches whoever is managing those offshore accounts.</div>
        <div class="dc14-ac"><button class="dc14-btn">Concur 517</button><button class="dc14-btn">Reply</button><button class="dc14-btn">Object 88</button></div>
      </div>
      <div class="dc14">
        <div class="dc14-hd"><div class="dc14-av" style="background:var(--purple);">TL</div><div class="dc14-hn">@trade.lawyer.dc</div><span class="dc14-bg" style="background:var(--purple-lt);border-color:rgba(45,26,74,0.2);color:var(--purple);">Trade Law</span></div>
        <div class="dc14-tx">The secondary sanctions architecture of 14404 mirrors Russia E.O. 14114. What happened there: European banks exited Russian transactions entirely rather than manage compliance risk. That didn't collapse Russia — it accelerated Chinese banks filling the gap. If the same pattern holds in Cuba, 14404 may inadvertently accelerate Chinese financial dominance in Havana. Worth watching.</div>
        <div class="dc14-ac"><button class="dc14-btn">Concur 203</button><button class="dc14-btn">Reply</button><button class="dc14-btn">Object 45</button></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sdiv14" style="margin-top:28px;"><span class="sdiv14-in">✦ ✦ ✦</span></div>
      <div style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:7.5px;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#b8860b;margin-bottom:6px;">◆ Civic Voice</div>
      <h2 style="font-family:'Libre Baskerville',serif;font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#1b3a6b;margin:0 0 20px;padding-bottom:12px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(27,58,107,0.09);">Your Take</h2>
      <div class="bt-card">
        <div class="bt-eyebrow">◆ How do you see it?</div>
        <div class="bt-perspective-grid" id="bt-perspectives">
          <button type="button" class="bt-perspective-btn" data-type="empathy"        data-label="🤝 With empathy">🤝 With empathy</button>
          <button type="button" class="bt-perspective-btn" data-type="practical"      data-label="🔧 Practical fix">🔧 Practical fix</button>
          <button type="button" class="bt-perspective-btn" data-type="accountability" data-label="📋 Hold accountable">📋 Hold accountable</button>
          <button type="button" class="bt-perspective-btn" data-type="opposition"     data-label="↔ Different view">↔ Different view</button>
        </div>
        <textarea class="bt-textarea" id="bt-body" rows="4" placeholder="What does <%= j eo_ref %> mean for your community? Be specific — your take is more persuasive when it's grounded in real experience."></textarea>
        <div class="bt-form-row">
          <div><input class="bt-input" type="text" id="bt-name" placeholder="Your occupation (optional)"></div>
          <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;"><div class="bt-checkbox-row" style="margin-bottom:0;"><input type="checkbox" id="bt-anon"><label for="bt-anon">Post anonymously</label></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="photo-attach-wrap">
          <span class="photo-attach-label">📷 Add a photo (optional)</span>
          <div class="photo-attach-options">
            <label class="photo-opt-btn"><input type="file" accept="image/*" style="display:none;" onchange="handleFileUpload(this, 'qa-eo')">📁 Camera roll</label>
            <span class="photo-opt-divider">or</span>
            <input type="text" class="photo-link-input" placeholder="🔗 Paste a photo link" oninput="handleLinkInput(this, 'qa-eo')">
          </div>
          <div class="photo-preview-area" id="preview-qa-eo" style="display:none;">
            <img class="photo-preview-img" id="preview-img-qa-eo" src="" alt="Preview" onclick="openLightbox(this.src)">
            <div class="photo-preview-meta"><span class="photo-preview-name" id="preview-name-qa-eo"></span><button class="photo-remove-btn" onclick="clearPhoto('qa-eo')">✕ Remove</button></div>
